# wikidata-prune.zph - cut a full Wikidata network down to a size an
# ordinary machine can hold.
#
# Run it against a freshly imported dump and save the result:
#
#   .load wikidata-20260309-all.json.bz2
#   .import examples/wikidata/wikidata-prune
#   .save wikidata-20260309-all-pruned.bin
#
# The goal is NOT a smaller copy of Wikidata. It is a network that still
# answers the questions people ask locally -- the class hierarchy, places,
# organizations, works, professions -- while leaving behind the bulk imports
# that make Wikidata large and that nobody queries offline: astronomical
# catalogues, sequence databases, cell lines, one item per integer, and the
# Wikimedia project's own bookkeeping pages.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two properties of the tools decide how this file has to be written.
# Both were verified against the engine; neither is obvious.
#
# 1. `.prune-nodes A P31 Qx` matches DIRECT instances of Qx only. It does not
#    descend the class hierarchy. Pruning `Q523` (star) therefore leaves every
#    instance of `Q2247863` (high proper-motion star, a subclass of Q523)
#    where it is -- which is why the 2026-03-09 pruned network still carried
#    2.2 million astronomical objects. A class named here must be a class that
#    actually CARRIES instances, not the concept above it.
#
#    There IS a form that descends -- `.prune-nodes A (A P31 C, C P279∗ Qx)`,
#    a conjunction whose named variable says which bindings die -- and it
#    deliberately is not used below. The class list is not only a way to
#    reach the instances: it is what the reach of this script was MEASURED
#    against, class by class, including the nine entries that had to be
#    withdrawn because their instances are themselves classes (see the
#    "checked and deliberately not pruned" block). A closure over P279 from
#    one root would delete a set nobody has counted, on a run that takes a
#    night and cannot be undone. Use it for a NEW domain by measuring it
#    first, the way the list below was measured.
#
# 2. `#` starts a comment only at the BEGINNING of a line. A trailing comment
#    is not stripped: on a dot-command it becomes part of the argument and the
#    command silently matches nothing, and on a fact line it becomes further
#    OBJECTS of the fact. Every comment here is therefore a full line.
#
# A third one is about the pattern itself: the single-fact form of
# `.prune-nodes` takes exactly ONE variable, because with two it could not say
# which side to delete. The four lines this file used to carry in the form
# `.prune-nodes A P131 B` were rejected on every run and never did anything;
# they are `.prune-facts` now, which drops the statements and keeps the
# entities. (The conjunction form of point 1 answers the same question by
# naming the variable, but says nothing about which statements to keep, so
# `.prune-facts` remains what those four lines mean.)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How to bring the class list up to date for a new dump, in two commands on
# the network you are about to prune:
#
#   .list-predicate-usage 60
#   .list-predicate-value-usage P31 300
#
# The first shows which predicates carry the mass, the second which classes
# do. Resolve the IDs to labels with
# `%(print (zelph/name (zelph/resolve "Q…" "wikidata") "en"))` and add what
# belongs to a domain below. The top 300 classes covered 80 % of all P31
# statements on 2026-03-09, so this list is worth re-deriving rather than
# inheriting.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

.lang wikidata
.remove-rules

# --- Astronomy -------------------------------------------------------------
# The one lever that does not depend on the class list: 96 % of all P6259
# ("epoch") statements name J2000, and nothing but an astronomical object has
# an epoch. This removes ~2.5 million objects in one pattern, whichever of the
# 60-odd catalogue classes they were filed under.
.prune-nodes A P6259 Q1264450

# The classes remain as a safety net for objects that carry no epoch. They are
# ordered by how many instances they had on 2026-03-09.
.prune-nodes A P31 Q1931185
.prune-nodes A P31 Q2247863
